"Microsoft Word - Update 106 JMIC Advisory Note 18 October_Final (https://www.ukmto.org/-/media/ukmto/products/update-106-jmic-advisory-note-18-october.pdf?rev=86f67da1821f4dcdb4e27868b6679729)\n\ue200cite\ue202turn13view0\ue201 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 339\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: 1. Confirmed Maritime Security Incidents List since last report\nL2@P0: Date Vessel Vessel\nL3@P0: Type\nL4@P0: Event\nL5@P0: Type Location Narrative\nL6@P0: 16 Oct 2026\nL7@P0: UKMTO 174-26 M/T ST3 Tanker Attack Central Arabian Gulf\nL8@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL9@P0: 16 Oct a tanker identified as the\nL10@P0: ST3 by the Indian shipping\nL11@P0: Minister was struck by an\nL12@P0: unknown projectile approx. 68\nL13@P0: NM northeast of Al Jubayl, Saudi\nL14@P0: Arabia. All crew reported safe and\nL15@P0: evacuated. No environmental\nL16@P0: impact reported.\nL17@P0: 17 Oct 2026\nL18@P0: UKMTO 175-26\nL19@P0: Name\nL20@P0: withheld UFN Tanker Attack Central Arabian Gulf\nL21@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL22@P0: 16 Oct a tanker was attacked after\nL23@P0: being approached by a small boat,\nL24@P0: resulting in damage to the aft port\nL25@P0: quarter. All crew accounted for\nL26@P0: and reported safe. Full damage\nL27@P0: assessment and environmental\nL28@P0: impact unknown.\nL29@P0: 17 Oct 2026\nL30@P0: UKMTO 176-26\nL31@P0: Name\nL32@P0: withheld UFN\nL33@P0: LNG\nL34@P0: tanker Attack Gulf of Aden\nL35@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL36@P0: 17 Oct an LNG tanker approx.\nL37@P0: 140 NM southeast of Al Mukalla,\nL38@P0: Yemen was approached by a skiff\nL39@P0: with seven persons on board,\nL40@P0: which fired two shots at the vessel.\nL41@P0: The embarked armed security\nL42@P0: team fired two warning shots and\nL43@P0: the skiff altered course.\nL44@P0: 18 Oct 2026\nL45@P0: UKMTO 177-26\nL46@P0: Name\nL47@P0: withheld UFN Tug Attack Strait of Hormuz\nL48@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL49@P0: 18 Oct a tug travelling south 20\nL50@P0: NM northeast of Khor Fakkan\nL51@P0: reported being struck by two\nL52@P0: unknown projectiles. The vessel\nL53@P0: sustained damage but continued\nL54@P0: its route. One minor crew injury\nL55@P0: has been reported.\nL56@P0: ICOD: 181300 UTC\nL57@P0: Joint Maritime Information Center\nL58@P0: Update 106 to JMIC Advisory Note:\nL59@P0: 01 March \u2013 18 October 2026\nL60@P0: Regional Tension - Impact on Maritime Security\nL61@P0: Area of Concern:\nL62@P0: Middle East Maritime Region \u2013\nL63@P0-1: JMIC# 001 26 Regional Threat Level: SEVERE2\nL64@P1: 2. Operational Summary \u2013 Last 72 Hours\nL65@P1: 2A. Arabian Gulf\nL66@P1: Two attacks were reported to UKMTO in this period, both in the central Arabian Gulf off Al\nL67@P1: Jubayl, Saudi Arabia (UKMTO 174-26 and 175-26). NAVAREA IX 382/26 reported a lifeboat\nL68@P1: adrift at 27-46.90N 050-36.00E.\nL69@P1: Traffic flows continued, with anchorage congestion high across the region.\nL70@P1: Navigational system interference rose on 16 Oct across the central and southern Arabian Gulf, from\nL71@P1: the Kuwait/north-east Saudi coast via Bahrain and Qatar to Abu Dhabi \u2013 Dubai.\nL72@P1: 2B. Strait of Hormuz\nL73@P1: One attack was reported in this period (UKMTO 177-26). UKMTO\u2019s weekly overview of 16\nL74@P1: October recorded 12 attacks in the week. NAVAREA IX 373/26 remains active for the GFS Pride\nL75@P1: wreck and floating containers. On 17 Oct the IRGC Navy claimed that two supertankers had struck\nL76@P1: mines in the Strait of Hormuz; there is no substantiation to these claims.\nL77@P1: IRGC attacks, attempted attacks, and/or harassment activity persisted including UAV overflight,\nL78@P1: targeted surveillance of merchant shipping, and occasional VHF hailing. These actions continue to\nL79@P1: demonstrate Iran\u2019s intent to assert presence along key transit lanes and maintain pressure on\nL80@P1: transiting vessels.\nL81@P1: Commercial traffic through the Strait of Hormuz remained at reduced levels compared to the 2025\nL82@P1: baseline. Independent tracking data indicated single digit transits, while US NCAGS data indicated\nL83@P1: US assisted traffic flows averaging approximately 28 vessel transits per day for the last 72 hours.\nL84@P1: UKMTO\u2019s weekly overview recorded 254 transits (125 outbound, 129 inbound) in the week to 14\nL85@P1: October, approximately 70% below pre-conflict levels.\nL86@P1: 2C. Gulf of Oman\nL87@P1: No new attack or disruptions were reported in this period. NAVAREA IX 380/26 (16 Oct) reported\nL88@P1: that the bulk carrier CAPE DAO (UKMTO 142-26) was sinking and drifting eastward approx. 33\nL89@P1: NM northeast of Port Al Suwaiq, cancelling NAVAREA IX 370/26; she has not sunk.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - Update 106 JMIC Advisory Note 18 October_Final (https://www.ukmto.org/-/media/ukmto/products/update-106-jmic-advisory-note-18-october.pdf?rev=86f67da1821f4dcdb4e27868b6679729)\n\ue200cite\ue202turn13view1\ue201 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 339\nL0@P0: 1\nL1@P0: 1. Confirmed Maritime Security Incidents List since last report\nL2@P0: Date Vessel Vessel\nL3@P0: Type\nL4@P0: Event\nL5@P0: Type Location Narrative\nL6@P0: 16 Oct 2026\nL7@P0: UKMTO 174-26 M/T ST3 Tanker Attack Central Arabian Gulf\nL8@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL9@P0: 16 Oct a tanker identified as the\nL10@P0: ST3 by the Indian shipping\nL11@P0: Minister was struck by an\nL12@P0: unknown projectile approx. 68\nL13@P0: NM northeast of Al Jubayl, Saudi\nL14@P0: Arabia. All crew reported safe and\nL15@P0: evacuated. No environmental\nL16@P0: impact reported.\nL17@P0: 17 Oct 2026\nL18@P0: UKMTO 175-26\nL19@P0: Name\nL20@P0: withheld UFN Tanker Attack Central Arabian Gulf\nL21@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL22@P0: 16 Oct a tanker was attacked after\nL23@P0: being approached by a small boat,\nL24@P0: resulting in damage to the aft port\nL25@P0: quarter. All crew accounted for\nL26@P0: and reported safe. Full damage\nL27@P0: assessment and environmental\nL28@P0: impact unknown.\nL29@P0: 17 Oct 2026\nL30@P0: UKMTO 176-26\nL31@P0: Name\nL32@P0: withheld UFN\nL33@P0: LNG\nL34@P0: tanker Attack Gulf of Aden\nL35@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL36@P0: 17 Oct an LNG tanker approx.\nL37@P0: 140 NM southeast of Al Mukalla,\nL38@P0: Yemen was approached by a skiff\nL39@P0: with seven persons on board,\nL40@P0: which fired two shots at the vessel.\nL41@P0: The embarked armed security\nL42@P0: team fired two warning shots and\nL43@P0: the skiff altered course.\nL44@P0: 18 Oct 2026\nL45@P0: UKMTO 177-26\nL46@P0: Name\nL47@P0: withheld UFN Tug Attack Strait of Hormuz\nL48@P0: UKMTO received a report that on\nL49@P0: 18 Oct a tug travelling south 20\nL50@P0: NM northeast of Khor Fakkan\nL51@P0: reported being struck by two\nL52@P0: unknown projectiles. The vessel\nL53@P0: sustained damage but continued\nL54@P0: its route. One minor crew injury\nL55@P0: has been reported.\nL56@P0: ICOD: 181300 UTC\nL57@P0: Joint Maritime Information Center\nL58@P0: Update 106 to JMIC Advisory Note:\nL59@P0: 01 March \u2013 18 October 2026\nL60@P0: Regional Tension - Impact on Maritime Security\nL61@P0: Area of Concern:\nL62@P0: Middle East Maritime Region \u2013\nL63@P0-1: JMIC# 001 26 Regional Threat Level: SEVERE2\nL64@P1: 2. Operational Summary \u2013 Last 72 Hours\nL65@P1: 2A. Arabian Gulf\nL66@P1: Two attacks were reported to UKMTO in this period, both in the central Arabian Gulf off Al\nL67@P1: Jubayl, Saudi Arabia (UKMTO 174-26 and 175-26). NAVAREA IX 382/26 reported a lifeboat\nL68@P1: adrift at 27-46.90N 050-36.00E.\nL69@P1: Traffic flows continued, with anchorage congestion high across the region.\nL70@P1: Navigational system interference rose on 16 Oct across the central and southern Arabian Gulf, from\nL71@P1: the Kuwait/north-east Saudi coast via Bahrain and Qatar to Abu Dhabi \u2013 Dubai.\nL72@P1: 2B. Strait of Hormuz\nL73@P1: One attack was reported in this period (UKMTO 177-26). UKMTO\u2019s weekly overview of 16\nL74@P1: October recorded 12 attacks in the week. NAVAREA IX 373/26 remains active for the GFS Pride\nL75@P1: wreck and floating containers. On 17 Oct the IRGC Navy claimed that two supertankers had struck\nL76@P1: mines in the Strait of Hormuz; there is no substantiation to these claims.\nL77@P1: IRGC attacks, attempted attacks, and/or harassment activity persisted including UAV overflight,\nL78@P1: targeted surveillance of merchant shipping, and occasional VHF hailing. These actions continue to\nL79@P1: demonstrate Iran\u2019s intent to assert presence along key transit lanes and maintain pressure on\nL80@P1: transiting vessels.\nL81@P1: Commercial traffic through the Strait of Hormuz remained at reduced levels compared to the 2025\nL82@P1: baseline. Independent tracking data indicated single digit transits, while US NCAGS data indicated\nL83@P1: US assisted traffic flows averaging approximately 28 vessel transits per day for the last 72 hours.\nL84@P1: UKMTO\u2019s weekly overview recorded 254 transits (125 outbound, 129 inbound) in the week to 14\nL85@P1: October, approximately 70% below pre-conflict levels.\nL86@P1: 2C. Gulf of Oman\nL87@P1: No new attack or disruptions were reported in this period. NAVAREA IX 380/26 (16 Oct) reported\nL88@P1: that the bulk carrier CAPE DAO (UKMTO 142-26) was sinking and drifting eastward approx. 33\nL89@P1: NM northeast of Port Al Suwaiq, cancelling NAVAREA IX 370/26; she has not sunk.\nL90@P1: Naval presence remained elevated due to blockade enforcement activity. According to the latest\nL91@P1: official CENTCOM figures (17 October), 138 commercial vessels had been redirected; four vessels\nL92@P1: had been disabled as of 10 \u2013 11 October during blockade enforcement. No further CENTCOM\nL93@P1: blockade enforcement action was announced in this period.\nL94@P1: Vessels were expected to respond promptly to military queries and maintain AIS-on unless\nL95@P1: constrained by security threats. Operators continued to exercise caution due to regional strike\nL96@P1: activity and ongoing IRGC monitoring of shipping in adjacent waters.\nL97@P1: 2D. Arabian Sea (No change)\nL98@P1: No confirmed attacks or disruptions were reported in this period. Naval forces continued routine\nL99@P1: patrols, and commercial traffic was transiting without disruption. The broader operating\nL100@P1: environment remained influenced by regional tensions.\nL101@P1: 2E. Gulf of Aden\nL102@P1: One incident was reported in this period (UKMTO 176-26): on 17 Oct an LNG tanker was\nL103@P1: approached by a skiff with seven persons on board approx. 140 NM southeast of Al Mukalla,\nL104@P1: Yemen; the skiff fired two shots and altered course after the armed security team fired two warning\nL105@P1: shots. NAVAREA IX 383/26 reported the event as an attempted piracy and advised mariners to\nL106@P1-2: keep clear. Commercial traffic remained relatively steady in the Gulf of Aden. 3\nL107@P2: 2F. Southern Red Sea & Bab el Mandeb (No change)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - Update 106 JMIC Advisory Note 18 October_Final (https://www.ukmto.org/-/media/ukmto/products/update-106-jmic-advisory-note-18-october.pdf?rev=86f67da1821f4dcdb4e27868b6679729)\n\ue200cite\ue202turn13view2\ue201 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn8view0\",\"lineno\":120}); Total lines: 339\nL102@P1: One incident was reported in this period (UKMTO 176-26): on 17 Oct an LNG tanker was\nL103@P1: approached by a skiff with seven persons on board approx. 140 NM southeast of Al Mukalla,\nL104@P1: Yemen; the skiff fired two shots and altered course after the armed security team fired two warning\nL105@P1: shots. NAVAREA IX 383/26 reported the event as an attempted piracy and advised mariners to\nL106@P1-2: keep clear. Commercial traffic remained relatively steady in the Gulf of Aden. 3\nL107@P2: 2F. Southern Red Sea & Bab el Mandeb (No change)\nL108@P2: No confirmed attacks or disruptions were reported in this period. Traffic remains below the 20 July\nL109@P2: Houthi selective embargo. Commercial traffic remained relatively steady in the Bab el Mandeb.\nL110@P2: 2G. Somali Coast and Somali Basin (No change)\nL111@P2: No confirmed attacks or disruptions were reported in this period. One merchant vessel has remained\nL112@P2: hijacked since the date of initial incident reporting:\nL113@P2: \uf0b7 MT ASANA, a Tanzanian-flagged product/chemical tanker, was hijacked on 17 July 2026\nL114@P2: approximately 65 NM southwest of Mukalla, Yemen, while carrying oil products.\nL115@P2: 2H. Northern Red Sea & Suez Canal\nL116@P2: No confirmed attacks or disruptions were reported in this period. On 16 Oct Saudi Arabia\u2019s Ministry\nL117@P2: of Defence reported intercepting a ballistic missile targeting Yanbu and two cruise missiles over the\nL118@P2: Red Sea. Ship loadings have resumed at Yanbu after strikes on the East \u2013 West Pipeline on 10 \u2013 11\nL119@P2: Sep, indicating the pipeline is functioning again.\nL120@P2: 2I. Eastern Mediterranean (No change)\nL121@P2: No confirmed attacks or disruptions were reported in this period \u2013 consistent with the persistent\nL122@P2-3: baseline. 4\nL123@P3: 3. Regional Threat Overview\nL124@P3: JMIC Threat Scale: \uc36a Low | \uc369 Moderate | \uc368 Substantial | \uc368 Severe | \ub6d1 Critical\nL125@P3: Region Threat Level Notes\nL126@P3: Arabian Gulf SUBSTANTIAL\nL127@P3: Due to recent Iranian attacks, four since 07 Oct, the threat level in the\nL128@P3: Arabian Gulf has increased to SUBSTANTIAL. Navigation interference has\nL129@P3: also increased. An attack is a strong possibility.\nL130@P3: Strait of Hormuz SEVERE\nL131@P3: Due to recent Iranian attacks, the threat level remains SEVERE, with\nL132@P3: deliberate hostile action considered as highly likely under current conditions.\nL133@P3: Navigation interference persists, and mariners are advised to remain vigilant\nL134@P3: for attempted mining within and adjacent to the TSS. IRGC attacks, hailing,\nL135@P3: and routing pressure continue, particularly for AIS-on vessels.\nL136@P3: Gulf of Oman MODERATE\nL137@P3: The U.S. naval blockade remains enforced, with ongoing monitoring,\nL138@P3: hailing, and verification queries, particularly near approaches to Iranian\nL139@P3: ports. Enforcement activity poses no direct impact to neutral shipping transits\nL140@P3: in the Gulf of Oman. Electronic interference continues to present a credible\nL141@P3: concern for mariners.\nL142@P3: Arabian Sea MODERATE Residual conflict-linked risk persists, but no attacks have been reported, and\nL143@P3: current indicators suggest no imminent change to the threat environment.\nL144@P3: Gulf of Aden SUBSTANTIAL\nL145@P3: Due to piracy-related events and Houthi rhetoric and attacks, the threat level\nL146@P3: remains SUBSTANTIAL, with a strong possibility of a maritime security\nL147@P3: incident in coastal approaches, transit corridors, and established small-craft\nL148@P3: areas.\nL149@P3: Bab el-Mandeb /\nL150@P3: Southern Red Sea SUBSTANTIAL\nL151@P3: Due to Houthi attacks on vessels and maritime infrastructure in the Southern\nL152@P3: Red Sea and territorial gains in Yemen along the Red Sea, the threat level\nL153@P3: remains SUBSTANTIAL. Based on the recent attacks, sustained intent\nL154@P3: against Saudi-affiliated interests, and maintained capability, additional\nL155@P3: attacks are a strong possibility\nL156@P3: Somali Coast /\nL157@P3: Somali Basin MODERATE PAG activity remains possible; monsoon conditions restrict offshore skiffs,\nL158@P3: but coastal opportunism persists.\nL159@P3: Northern Red Sea /\nL160@P3: Suez Canal MODERATE Suez Canal operations remain normal. Periodic Electro-magnetic\nL161@P3: Interference (EMI) remains possible.\nL162@P3: Eastern\nL163@P3: Mediterranean LOW Due to a lack of security incidents, the threat level remains LOW. Localized\nL164@P3: EMI disruptions persist, and regional naval presence remains consistent.\nL165@P3-4: JMIC Regional Threat Levels5\nL166@P4: 4. Maritime Incidents & Traffic Assessment\nL167@P4: 4A. Confirmed Vessel Incidents since 01 March\nL168@P4: Total of 153 incidents since 01 March; four incidents since the last report (UKMTO 174-26 to 177-\nL169@P4: 26).\nL170@P4: 4B. Infrastructure Incidents (Last 72 Hours)\nL171@P4: The overall risk remains MODERATE, driven by the potential for an increase in kinetic activity\nL172@P4: against ports, terminals and offshore installations along the Iranian, Yemeni and Saudi coasts. On\nL173@P4: 16 October Saudi forces intercepted a ballistic missile aimed at Yanbu (Saudi Ministry of Defence).\nL174@P4: No other attack on ports, terminals, offshore platforms, oil and gas loading facilities or subsea\nL175@P4: infrastructure was reported in this period.\nL176@P4: 4C. Traffic Trends\nL177@P4: \uf0b7 Strait of Hormuz:\nL178@P4: o Historical average: ~138 vessels/day (2025 baseline)\nL179@P4: o U.S. \u2013 facilitated SoH transits 15 \u2013 18 October (past 72 hours): 83\nL180@P4: * Metrics are drawn from two separate sources: U.S. NCAGS data reflecting U.S.-facilitated Strait of Hormuz transits\nL181@P4: only, and S&P Maritime/SeaVision data reflecting non-facilitated vessel traffic for cargo-carrying vessels above 1,000\nL182@P4: deadweight tons, excluding vessels calling Iranian ports adjacent to the Strait of Hormuz.\nL183@P4: \uf0b7 Bab el-Mandeb:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpread OAT / Bund aujourd'hui : 142.3 pb au 19/10/2026 (https://www.ideal-investisseur.fr/marches/spread-oat-bund.html)\n\ue